\p{On a given continuous space, does the discrete physics approach the
continuous one? The [spatial Dirac calculation](fdip-000T) used lattice
spacing one. Now supply a circle and place the graph on it. There are
finitely many sites; the field values may still be real. For a field that
changes over many vertices, the spacing is small relative to the scale
of that change. Dividing an edge difference by the spacing then suggests
a derivative. Continuous values concern the allowed field values, here
real numbers; differentiability concerns the variation of a field on the
supplied space and limits of difference quotients. Smoothness asks for
derivatives of every order.}

\p{The [circle calculation](fdip-004G) makes a limited comparison precise:
each fixed Fourier mode of the discrete operator approaches its counterpart
on the supplied circle. Approximation needs comparison maps and a limit;
the chapter states these together with its pairings, positive precision
and Gaussian moments. The circle is an input, so this result does not
construct continuous space from the graph. The finite Dirac kernel in
\ref{fdip-000T} depends on the parity of the periods; that separate
calculation cautions against inferring a general continuum theory from
one fixed-mode limit. Whether continuous-space physics can be recovered
at large scales without supplying the space remains open here.}

\transclude{fdip-004G}
